  <h1>Types of Dog Bite Claims</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Strict liability</h2><p>Over 35 states impose strict liability: the owner is automatically liable for bite injuries regardless of prior knowledge of the dog&rsquo;s dangerous propensity. No need to prove negligence or prior bad behavior. California, New York, Illinois, Texas, and Florida all have strict liability statutes (though details vary).</p>
  <h2>One-bite rule</h2><p>Several states (including some that call themselves strict liability but have significant exceptions) require showing the owner knew or should have known the dog was dangerous. One prior incident that put the owner on notice is often sufficient. States following this rule: Virginia, North Carolina, Georgia (partially).</p>
  <h2>Negligence</h2><p>Even without a strict liability statute, an owner may be negligent for failing to restrain a known dangerous dog, leaving a dog unleashed in violation of a local ordinance, or failing to warn visitors. Negligence per se arises when the owner violates a leash law and a bite occurs.</p>
  <h2>Premises liability</h2><p>When a dog bite occurs on commercial property, the property owner or manager may be liable for failing to prevent known dangerous animals on the premises. Relevant in cases involving guard dogs, dogs in apartment complexes, or dogs at businesses.</p>
